--
instantiating 'std::__enable_if_t' with <FALSE
--
N5008 [temp.inst]/2: member function templates of a class template are not
instantiated when the enclosing specialization is instantiated; their
signatures stay dependent on their own template parameters until odr-used.

CBMC concretizes std::unique_ptr<T>'s =delete'd deleter constructor template
during instantiation of unique_ptr<T> (substituting the default
_Del=deleter_type=default_delete<T> and evaluating the parameter type), which
demands std::enable_if_t<false>::type and hard-errors ("instantiating
std::__enable_if_t with <FALSE, default_delete&&>" at unique_ptr.h:354).  g++
and clang++ accept the program.

Root cause of the enable_if_t<false> dog-food failures in parse_options.cpp,
irep_serialization.cpp and ui_message.cpp (all hold std::unique_ptr members).
This is an instance of the deferred lazy member-function-template instantiation
work.  Flip to CORE once member function templates are kept dependent (not
concretized) during class-template instantiation.
